    <div class="photo_info container">
        <h6>
            <b> Photography</b><br><br> is the art and science of capturing images using light and a photosensitive
            surface, such as
            film or a digital sensor. It is a visual medium that has evolved significantly since its invention in the
            19th century. Here's some key information about photography:

            <br><br> <b> History:</b><br> Photography was invented in the early 19th century. The first permanent
            photograph, known as the
            "View from the Window at Le Gras," was taken by Joseph Nicéphore Niépce in 1826. The medium rapidly evolved
            with innovations like the daguerreotype and the calotype.

            <br><br> <b> Genres of Photography:</b>

            <br><br> <b> Portrait Photography:</b><br> Focuses on capturing the likeness, personality, and expressions
            of individuals or
            groups.

            <br><br> <b> Landscape Photography</b><br> Involves capturing scenic vistas, natural environments, and
            outdoor settings.

            <br><br> <b> Street Photography:</b> <br>Captures candid moments and everyday life in public places.

            <br><br> <b> Wildlife Photography:</b><br> Involves photographing animals in their natural habitats.

            <br><br> <b> Fashion Photography:</b><br> Showcases clothing, accessories, and models for the fashion
            industry.

            <br><br> <b> Documentary Photography:</b> <br>Tells a story or documents real-life events and issues.

            <br><br> <b> Macro Photography:</b><br> Captures extreme close-ups of small subjects, often revealing
            intricate details.

            <br><br> <b> Architecture Photography:</b><br> Focuses on buildings, structures, and architectural details.

            <br><br> <b> Astrophotography:</b><br>Involves capturing celestial objects and astronomical phenomena.
            Photographic Techniques:

            <br><br> <b> Exposure:</b><br> Controlling the amount of light that reaches the sensor or film to create a
            properly exposed
            image.

            <br><br> <b>Composition:</b><br> Arranging elements within the frame to create visually appealing and
            meaningful images.

            <br><br> <b> Lighting:</b> <br>Utilizing natural or artificial light sources to enhance or manipulate the
            appearance of subjects.

            <br><br> <b> Focus and Depth of Field:</b> <br>Selecting the point of focus and controlling the range of
            sharpness in an image.

            <br><br> <b>Shutter Speed:</b><br> Determining how long the camera's shutter remains open to capture motion
            or create specific
            effects.

            <br><br> <b> Aperture:</b> <br>Adjusting the size of the lens aperture to control the amount of light and
            depth of field.

            <br><br> <b> ISO Sensitivity:</b><br> Setting the sensitivity of the camera's sensor to control image noise
            in different lighting
            conditions.

            <br><br> <b> Photography as Art:</b> <br>Photography is considered both an art form and a means of visual
            communication. Many
            photographers use their work to convey emotions, tell stories, and explore creative concepts.

            <br><br> <b> Digital Editing:</b><br> Post-processing plays a significant role in modern photography.
            Software like Adobe
            Photoshop and Lightroom is used to enhance, retouch, and manipulate images to achieve the desired final
            result.

            <br><br> <b> Photography Careers:</b><br> Photography offers various career opportunities, including
            professional photographers,
            photojournalists, fashion photographers, commercial photographers, and fine art photographers.

            <br><br> <b> Photography Communities:</b><br> There are numerous photography communities, forums, and social
            media platforms
            where photographers share their work, exchange knowledge, and engage with others who share their passion.

            <br><br> <b> Ethical Considerations:</b><br> Ethical issues in photography include obtaining consent for
            portraits, respecting
            privacy, and representing subjects accurately and ethically in documentary work.

            <br> Photography is a diverse and dynamic medium that continues to evolve with advancements in technology
            and
            changes in artistic trends. It allows individuals to capture and convey the world around them in unique and
            meaningful ways.




        </h6>
